Essay Guidelines

Format: Your essays should be 5-6 pages, double-spaced, in 12-point Times font, with I inch margins. Do not push your luck with this, I know every trick in the book. Make sure your pages are numbered. Number them by hand if you don't know how to make your word processor do numbering.

Footnotes: Since you will be drawing on a limited source base, I would like you to use parenthetical citations, and not footnotes or endnotes. For example:

Suetonius, describing Caesar, writes that he was, "...tall of stature, with a fair complexion, shapely limbs, a somewhat full face, and keen black eyes..." (Bailkey, 373).

You need only have a citation if you are quoting directly, or if you are borrowing an idea.

Title Page: Your title page should have your student ID number, the name of your essay, the name of the class and the date. Do not put your name anywhere on the essay.

Plagiarism: If any part of your essay is presented as your own work, but is not, this is plagiarism. If you are going to borrow material from one of the books for the class, you must indicate clearly what book and page number it came from, per the guidelines above. Do not borrow material from any outside books, this should not be necessary. Do not borrow material from friends. If you are caught in violation of this policy, the penalties are severe. If you would like any more information about this policy, please let me know, and I will provide a copy of the UCLA guidelines governing plagiarism.

Primary Documents: History is about the use of primary documents. As such, your essay should rely mostly on the primary documents assigned for the class, and should reflect your familiarity with those documents. The primary sources for this class are the documents in the Bailkey reader, as well as the novels and plays The textbook is a secondary source, and does not count as a primary document, except in rare cases where it has a passage reprinted directly from some ancient text.

Due: Your essay should be turned in at the beginning of section, Week 5. If you have a legitimate need for an extension, you must bring this to my attention ASAP. The later you talk to me, the less likely I am to accept your excuse.

Spelling and Grammar: As previously noted, I interpret significant number of spelling and grammar errors as a sign that the student did not care about their essay. As such, I strongly encourage you to spell check your essay, and to read it thoroughly for errors of grammar. If you are not good at this, try to find a friend, or take advantage of College Tutorials (x61145), or bring it to my special office hours and I will read it.

Special Office Hours: I will hold extra office hours on Monday, October 30th, from 2:00 to 4:00 and on Tuesday, October 31st from 1:00 to 3:00. You may bring your essay to me at that time and I will give it as much time as is possible based on how many people show up. If your essay is not finished, you may bring what you have, or you may bring an outline of your ideas. You are also welcome to email me your thesis statement, and I will take a look at it. The earlier I get it, the greater the likelihood you will get useful feedback.

Thesis: These topics are rather more creative than your usual history essay. Nonetheless, every history essay, including these, should have a thesis statement. This is reasonably brief statement of the main point your essay is trying to make. A thesis should be an idea and an arguable point, not a statement of fact, or what is generally regarded as fact. Your thesis should also be a point that is provable, and not dependent on a value judgment. For example:

Julius Caesar was the first emperor of Rome. (Bad, because it is essentially an accepted fact)

Julius Caesar was the greatest of the Roman emperors. (Bad, unprovable value judgment)

Julius Caesar was able to become the emperor of Rome because of fundamental changes in Roman society that had occurred over the course of the century in which he lived. The creation of a landless population of Roman citizens had weakened the government, and allowed Caesar to gain their support with grants of land. Once the army had become the tool of generals, dictatorship was almost inevitable. (Acceptable thesis statement)

Etcetera: These essay topics ask you to take on a persona. Feel free to speak in the first person, even in character.

Essay Topics

1. You are the ruler of a great civilization, which we will call the Mortimerians, in honor of our esteemed professor. You have decided to present your subjects with a law code. In your introduction, explain your basic goals for your law code, and how those goals reflect the needs of the Mortimerians. Then give me four or five laws (one or two sentences each) that you intend to include in your law code. For each law, explain why you are including it, and what problems you foresee with the law, if any. Also include two laws from other civilizations that you will not include, and explain why. You are a well-educated ruler, and you have done your research in the Great Library of Alexandria, so your law code should definitely reflect a familiarity with the law codes of other civilizations.

2. You are the high priest of the Mortimerians, familiar with the history, doctrines and deities of Mortimerian religion. Briefly introduce me to your civilization, and then tell me about your religion, its development, and its role within the civilization. Some questions you might think about: How do you, as high priest, relate to the king of the Mortimerians? Why is it that way? Is Mortimerian religion polytheistic, monotheistic, or something else? Why? What role do the gods play, and why do you think it is that way? Do you have any rituals, and what purpose do they serve? You may answer any or all of these questions, or questions of your own, but you should be making a clear statement about the relationship between the needs of a civilization and its religion.




2. Roman history is often organized around its leaders, who seem to dominate the historical landscape. Among them are the brothers Gracchi, Julius Caesar, Augustus, Agricola; Marcus Aurelius and Diocletian. Imagine you are a newspaper editor in Rome, and write an editorial in which you discuss one of these leaders, commenting on their policy, their rise to power, their character, or whatever else you feel is appropriate. You should write from the perspective of some constituency; the old aristocracy, the equestrians, the plebeians, or some other Roman social/political group. Make sure to specify in your introduction which group you represent. Your main goal should be to indicate (based on the perspective you choose, as well as the circumstances the leader faced) if the leader's actions were good or bad for Rome.




